# Expectation record for 05_pointers/007_casts_roundtrip.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 007_casts_roundtrip
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Object-pointer round trips through integer carriers wide enough to hold them
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
No conversion narrows: every carrier is at least as wide as a pointer and the bridge type
back to a pointer is exactly pointer width, and both width relations are checked at run
time rather than assumed.  Each round-tripped pointer is compared and dereferenced only
after being restored to its original type, so no object is accessed through an
incompatible type.  Neither a pointer nor a carrier value is ever printed.
Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Converting a pointer to an integer and back is implementation-defined; the standard
guarantees the result only for a round trip through void pointer.  The program therefore
prints no pointer or carrier value at all and asserts only equality with the original,
the value of the object reached, and offsets - facts that hold on every target that can
represent a pointer in the carrier, which the program itself verifies.  uintptr_t is not
used because it is an optional typedef and would require a header.
END
